 Dear Group,
I am trying to overlay two rasters using ggplot2 and require one of them to be semi-transparent. Basically I have a hillShade raster which is computed from an elevation data raster, and I wish to overlay the elevation raster onto the hillshade raster so the resulting plot doesn't look 'flat'. You can see what I mean in the code below.

Using base graphics this is quite easy by specifying the fill colour of the hillshade raster as greyscale and then overlaying the elevation raster with the fill colour set to vary according to the altitude and setting the alpha transparency to say 0.4 so that the hillShade underneath is visible (example also in code below). I can plot each of the rasters nicely enough by themselves but I cannot seem to plot both together, one on top of the other. I think it is because I do not understand colour scale and aesthetics mapping. Apologies in advance if this kind of question has been answered before, but I have looked here and elsewhere, but can't seem to find something to help. I would also be very grateful if someone can point out a better way of specifying the colour scheme so that I do not have a legend running down the side of my plot with all 100 colours on it (you can see what I mean running the code below).

Sorry of the example seems a bit verbose, but I wanted to illustrate what I hope to achieve, and what I have managed so far in the hope someone will be able to help.

Many thanks in advance,

Simon

